<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.13.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01488</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.13.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>947d5e35-a252-4d4b-8138-3682ad62e2b0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_2db26d10-04e4-4540-a272-30228d06b376" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Here is a graph of \(p\), an insect population, \(w\) weeks after it was first measured. The population
          grows exponentially. 
          <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_10_01.jpg"/></media></figure><para>
              What is the weekly factor of growth for the insect population?</para>
         </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.13.2. 
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 3.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e8f10c0a-032d-4ab5-8933-ce06c9f51e2d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>What was the population when it was first measured?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.13.2. 
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 300.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="cbfdb135-dc79-4528-bb35-0db6647ca15f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Write an equation relating \(p\) and \(w\).</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(p=300+3^w\)</para>
            <para>\(p=300 \cdot 3^w\)</para>
            <para>\(p= \frac{300}{3^w}\)</para>
            <para>\(p=-300 \cdot 3^w\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.13.2. 
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(p=300 \cdot 3^w\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_81c70864-a6bf-4d9e-83de-c1d4540d165c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Here is a graph of the function \(f\) defined by \(f(x)=a \cdot b^x\). <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_10_02.jpg"/></media></figure><emphasis>
            Select three </emphasis>possible values of \(b\).</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.13.2.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(\frac{1}{10}\), \(\frac12\), and \(\frac{9}{10}\).
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_20429ef0-bf0e-4a6b-8570-8d3531211391" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The function \(f\) is given by \(f(x)=50\cdot (\frac{1}{2})^x\), and the function \(g\) is given by
          \(g(x)=50 \cdot (\frac{1}{3})^x\).
          Here are graphs of \(f\) and \(g\).
          
          <figure><media alt="Graph of two decreasing exponential functions, xy-plane, origin O."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_10_03.svg"/></media></figure></para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.13.3 – 5.13.4. Are you using it
        the right way?
     </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Graph 1. The growth factor for \(f(x)\) is greater than that of \(g(x)\), so \(f(x)\)
        lies above \(g(x)\) on the graph.
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="bd2f7076-6229-4987-ad09-6ec00a25b88d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=50 \cdot 3^x\). The function \(g\) is defined by \(g(x)=a
          \cdot b^x\).
          Here are graphs of \(f\) and \(g\).
          
          <figure><media alt="Graph of two increasing exponential functions, labeled f and g, xy-plane, origin O."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_10_04.svg"/></media></figure><para>
              In \(g(x)\), \(a\) is greater than/less than 50.</para>
         </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is greater than. The \(y\)-intercept of \(g\) is above the \(y\)-intercept of \(f\).
     </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="cd5259b9-f0b7-48fb-a1a4-bc137b5b7781" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>In \(g(x)\), \(b\) is greater than/less than 3.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.13.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is less than. In \(g(x)\), \(b\) is less than 3 (but still larger than 1) because the growth
        rate of \(f\) is faster than the growth rate of \(g\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
